# TRAINING SCREEN

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Training Screen is the operational core of the Combat Athlete System.

It guides the athlete through one exercise, one set and one decision at a time.

The athlete should never need to remember:

- the next exercise;
- the target load;
- the target repetitions;
- the prescribed tempo;
- the required rest period;
- the current position inside the session.

CAS carries the cognitive load.

The athlete performs.

---

# Core Principle

One exercise.

One target.

One action.

Everything unrelated disappears.

The Training Screen should feel like an experienced coach standing beside the athlete.

Calm.

Precise.

Present.

---

# Primary Question

The Training Screen must always answer:

What do I do now?

It should also make the following information immediately available:

- what comes next;
- how much of the session is complete;
- what was prescribed;
- what was actually performed.

---

# Information Hierarchy

The screen follows this hierarchy:

1. Session progress
2. Current exercise
3. Current set
4. Target load
5. Target repetitions or duration
6. Execution parameters
7. Primary action
8. Next exercise preview
9. Secondary controls

Nothing should compete with the current exercise.

---

# Screen Header

The header provides orientation.

Recommended content:

- session title;
- session progress;
- elapsed session time when useful;
- pause or exit control.

The header remains compact.

It must not reduce the space available for the current exercise.

---

# Session Progress

The athlete should always know how much of the session is complete.

Progress should be shown as:

- a horizontal progress bar;
- a percentage;
- or both when space allows.

Example:

Session

42 %

The progress indicator should update after each validated set or completed work interval.

It should behave like an energy bar:

clear;

continuous;

easy to understand.

It must represent meaningful completion.

Not elapsed time alone.

---

# Current Exercise

The current exercise is the dominant content.

Recommended information:

- exercise name;
- exercise category when useful;
- current set;
- total number of sets;
- primary target;
- technical cue when necessary.

Example:

Tractions lestées

Série 3 sur 5

+20 kg

5 répétitions

The athlete should understand the prescription within three seconds.

---

# Exercise Name

The exercise name should use strong hierarchy.

Recommended style:

Heading 1 or Heading 2 depending on available space.

Avoid abbreviations unless universally understood by the target athlete.

Good:

Développé couché

Tractions lestées

Low kick au sac

Avoid:

DC

TL

LK Bag

unless the athlete has explicitly enabled compact terminology.

---

# Set Position

The current set position must remain visible.

Example:

Série 3 sur 5

For interval work:

Round 4 sur 8

For timed work:

Bloc 2 sur 4

Use terminology adapted to the exercise type.

---

# Load

Load is one of the most important values.

It should be large, clear and visually stronger than surrounding text.

Examples:

80 kg

+20 kg

Élastique moyen

Poids du corps

The unit must remain visible.

Never display a number without context.

---

# Repetitions

Repetitions should be visually paired with load.

Examples:

5 répétitions

8 par côté

Maximum technique

The athlete must never confuse:

- total repetitions;
- repetitions per side;
- repetitions per arm;
- repetitions remaining.

---

# Duration

For timed exercises, duration replaces repetitions as the primary target.

Examples:

30 s

2 min

5 × 3 min

The timer should become visually dominant only when timing is active.

---

# Tempo

Tempo is displayed only when prescribed.

Example:

3–1–X–1

A short explanation may be available through secondary disclosure.

Do not permanently overload the main screen with tempo education.

---

# RPE and RIR

RPE or RIR should appear only when relevant to the exercise prescription.

Examples:

RPE 8

2 répétitions en réserve

Never show both RPE and RIR unless there is a clear reason.

The terminology should remain consistent throughout the application.

---

# Technique Notes

Technique notes should remain short.

One cue at a time.

Good:

Poitrine haute.

Accélère dès le départ.

Garde les coudes sous la barre.

Avoid long paragraphs.

Detailed instructions may be accessible through a secondary action.

---

# Exercise Media

Video or animation may be available as optional support.

It should never autoplay during training.

It should never dominate the screen.

Recommended use:

- technique reminder;
- unfamiliar exercise;
- corrective cue;
- rehabilitation variation.

The athlete should be able to train without opening media.

---

# Primary Action

Only one Primary Button should dominate the screen.

Possible labels:

- Valider la série
- Démarrer
- Terminer le round
- Continuer
- Passer au repos
- Reprendre

The label should describe the immediate action.

Avoid generic labels such as:

OK

Suivant

Confirmer

when a more precise verb is available.

---

# Set Validation

After completing a set, the athlete should be able to record:

- actual load;
- actual repetitions;
- perceived effort;
- pain or limitation when relevant.

The default values should match the prescription.

The athlete should edit only when reality differs.

Minimize typing.

Prefer:

- step controls;
- quick selectors;
- previously used values;
- large touch targets.

---

# Automatic Defaults

CAS should prefill the expected performance.

Example:

Prescription:

80 kg × 5

Default recorded result:

80 kg × 5

The athlete validates with one action when the prescription was completed as planned.

This reduces interaction during training.

---

# Previous Performance

Previous performance may appear as discreet supporting information.

Example:

Dernière fois

77,5 kg × 5

It must remain secondary.

The athlete should not need to open history to remember the previous load.

---

# Next Exercise Preview

The next exercise should always be announced before the current transition.

Recommended content:

- next exercise name;
- expected load;
- repetitions or duration;
- equipment preparation when useful.

Example:

Ensuite

Développé militaire

42,5 kg × 6

This preview is especially important during rest periods.

The athlete should be able to prepare equipment in advance.

---

# Voice Guidance

Voice guidance may announce:

- current exercise;
- target load;
- target repetitions;
- next exercise;
- rest duration;
- final countdown;
- session progress milestones.

Example:

Prochain exercice : développé militaire.

Charge prévue : quarante-deux kilos et demi.

Six répétitions.

Voice guidance remains optional.

It should be concise.

Never motivational.

Never theatrical.

---

# Session Progression

The session advances through explicit states:

1. Exercise ready
2. Work in progress
3. Set completed
4. Rest
5. Next exercise ready
6. Session completed

Transitions must remain predictable.

The athlete should never wonder whether a set has been saved.

---

# Session State Persistence

The active session must survive:

- screen locking;
- application backgrounding;
- accidental navigation;
- temporary interruption.

The athlete should be able to resume exactly where they stopped.

The current exercise, set, timer and recorded data must be preserved.

---

# Screen Lock

The rest timer and session state must continue when the iPhone locks.

When the athlete returns:

- the timer reflects real elapsed time;
- the next action is immediately visible;
- no progress is lost.

CAS should not assume the screen remains active during the entire workout.

---

# Pause

Pause is available but secondary.

Pausing should stop:

- active work intervals when appropriate;
- session timing if the product definition requires it.

Pausing should not erase:

- current set;
- exercise state;
- recorded performance;
- rest progress.

The pause state must be visually unambiguous.

---

# Skip

Skipping an exercise or set is a secondary action.

CAS should ask for a reason only when that information will improve future recommendations.

Possible reasons:

- equipment unavailable;
- pain;
- time constraint;
- excessive fatigue;
- exercise not understood.

Avoid unnecessary confirmation dialogs.

---

# Modify Prescription

The athlete may adjust:

- load;
- repetitions;
- exercise variation;
- rest duration.

Adjustments should remain easy but not visually dominant.

CAS should distinguish:

- prescribed value;
- completed value.

This information may improve future recommendations.

---

# Pain and Safety

A pain or safety action should remain accessible.

Possible labels:

- Signaler une douleur
- Adapter l’exercice
- Arrêter l’exercice

CAS should never encourage the athlete to push through significant pain.

The tone remains calm and non-alarming.

---

# Exercise Types

The Training Screen must support several exercise families.

---

# Strength Exercise

Primary information:

- exercise;
- load;
- repetitions;
- sets;
- rest;
- RPE or RIR when prescribed.

---

# Bodyweight Exercise

Primary information:

- exercise;
- repetitions;
- variation;
- added load or assistance;
- rest.

Examples:

Poids du corps

+10 kg

Élastique léger

---

# Timed Exercise

Primary information:

- exercise;
- duration;
- intensity;
- work-to-rest ratio;
- rounds.

---

# Interval Exercise

Primary information:

- work duration;
- rest duration;
- round number;
- total rounds;
- next phase.

---

# Bag Work

Primary information:

- round duration;
- objective;
- strike family;
- intensity;
- rest;
- round number.

Example:

Sac lourd

Round 3 sur 6

3 min

Puissance maximale

Poings et low kicks

Technical instructions must remain short and specific.

---

# Combat Conditioning

Primary information:

- work interval;
- movement sequence;
- target intensity;
- recovery;
- number of rounds.

Avoid presenting complex combinations as dense paragraphs.

Break sequences into readable steps.

---

# Mobility and Recovery

Primary information:

- movement;
- duration;
- side;
- breathing or range cue.

The interface should become visually calmer.

No aggressive performance language.

---

# Supersets and Circuits

The screen should clearly show:

- current movement;
- position in the sequence;
- remaining movements;
- rest location.

Example:

Circuit 2 sur 4

Exercice 1 sur 3

The athlete should not confuse exercise progress with circuit progress.

---

# Progress Indicators

When several levels exist, distinguish them clearly:

- session progress;
- exercise progress;
- set or round progress.

Do not display three large progress bars simultaneously.

Only the most relevant level should dominate.

---

# Layout

Recommended vertical structure:

Compact session header

Session progress

Current exercise

Primary prescription

Technique cue

Primary action

Previous performance

Next exercise preview

Secondary controls

The primary action should remain reachable without excessive scrolling.

---

# One-Handed Use

The Training Screen should support one-handed interaction when possible.

Primary controls should remain in the lower half of the screen.

Avoid placing frequent actions exclusively in the top navigation area.

---

# Touch Targets

Minimum touch area:

44 × 44 pt

Preferred during training:

48 × 48 pt

Controls must remain usable with:

- sweaty hands;
- gloves removed quickly;
- fatigue;
- reduced precision.

---

# Typography

Recommended hierarchy:

Session title:

caption or body

Exercise name:

heading1 or heading2

Set position:

caption or body

Load:

display or heading1

Repetitions:

heading1 or heading2

Technique cue:

body

Previous performance:

caption

Next exercise:

heading3 or bodyLarge

Primary button:

body with Medium weight

Timers:

display

Numbers must remain easy to compare.

---

# Color Usage

Background:

Graphite Black

Exercise surface:

Charcoal or Steel Gray

Primary action:

Performance Orange

Completion:

Recovery Green

Warning:

Warning Amber

Pain or critical state:

Critical Red

Do not use orange for every active element.

Do not use green for ordinary navigation.

---

# Icons

Icons remain optional.

Possible uses:

- timer;
- load;
- repetitions;
- pause;
- audio;
- pain;
- completion.

Icons never replace essential labels.

Avoid decorative exercise illustrations.

---

# Motion

Motion explains state changes.

Allowed:

- progress update;
- transition from work to rest;
- subtle validation feedback;
- timer state transition;
- restrained button press feedback.

Not allowed:

- bouncing values;
- repeated pulsing;
- flashing countdown;
- dramatic exercise transitions;
- celebratory effects after every set.

Respect Reduced Motion.

---

# Haptics

Recommended haptic moments:

- work interval starts;
- set validated;
- final rest countdown;
- rest completed;
- session completed;
- critical warning.

Avoid haptics for ordinary scrolling or navigation.

Haptics should reinforce meaningful state changes.

---

# Sound

CAS remains silent by default unless voice or sound guidance is enabled.

Optional sounds may signal:

- start;
- final countdown;
- rest complete;
- session complete.

Sounds must remain short and functional.

---

# Accessibility

The screen must support:

- Dynamic Type;
- VoiceOver;
- Reduced Motion;
- high contrast;
- large touch targets;
- logical focus order.

VoiceOver should announce:

- exercise name;
- current set;
- target load;
- target repetitions;
- current state;
- primary action.

Important information must not rely on color alone.

---

# Error States

Possible errors:

- session cannot be saved;
- timer interrupted;
- data unavailable;
- recommendation missing.

The screen must explain:

- what happened;
- whether training can continue;
- what the athlete should do.

Never discard completed work silently.

---

# Offline Behaviour

The Training Screen should remain functional without a network connection when the session has already been generated.

The athlete must be able to:

- view the session;
- complete exercises;
- record results;
- run timers;
- finish the session.

Synchronization may occur later.

---

# Do

- show one exercise at a time;
- prefill prescribed values;
- keep the primary action obvious;
- announce the next exercise;
- preserve progress when the screen locks;
- make timers reliable;
- keep technique cues short;
- distinguish prescribed and completed values;
- support combat-specific intervals and bag work.

---

# Do Not

- display the entire session as the primary training interface;
- show unrelated statistics during a set;
- require repeated typing;
- hide the next exercise;
- stop timers when the phone locks;
- use dramatic animations;
- overload the screen with technical explanations;
- make the athlete remember loads or repetitions;
- depend on a permanent network connection.

---

# Success Criteria

The Training Screen is successful when:

- the athlete knows what to do within three seconds;
- the current target is readable at arm's length;
- the next exercise is known before transition;
- a completed set can be recorded with one action when performed as prescribed;
- the session survives screen locking and interruptions;
- progress is always clear;
- the athlete spends more attention on execution than on the application.

---

# Final Principle

CAS remembers the plan.

CAS tracks the session.

CAS announces what comes next.

The athlete only has to perform.